 those of us who have a  lot of our listeners who will be very  amateur athletes  still kind of daydreaming of gold and if  not  gold but just at least of getting a good  deal better  give us your magic nostrum if you could  i think a lot of it is not just the  physical part but i think it's  mentally how you position yourself in  life  and what kind of uh person you're going  to be  i think in a lot of cases you're not  going to be a better athlete  you're not going to be much a much more  better athlete than the type of person  that you  are it's about personal development and  if you're able to  to develop yourself academically or in  your job  or or or just be more receptive to  information because you're willing to  work hard in sports i think those are  the benefits  uh that everyone can get from sports it  has nothing to do with how  fast you are or or or what to what level  you go  those are life skills that can go a long  way  dr edward moses thank you very much for  joining us  and thank you very much this interview  is from our show  the economist asks and every week we  publish a new episode  in which we interview key figures in  world events  the link opposite will take you to a  page with all of our previous episodes  thanks for watching
 

